Hold the microphone's gain through the pauses between words

The legacy gain controller (AGC1, which Fogata's voice engine uses on the desktop in its
adaptive analog mode) has a gate in its digital part (WebRtcAgc_ComputeDigitalGains,
agc/legacy/digital_agc.cc): in a pause between words, once its fast envelope falls well
below the level the gain is set from (the slow envelope, which decays slowly after
speech), it keeps only (178 + gain_adj) / 256 of the gain above the table's lowest, down
to 178 / 256 (3.2 dB less), and lets it back as the next word starts. It runs in every AGC1
mode. The room's noise therefore sank about 2 dB in each pause and came back with each
word: measured in the call lab (mic.sh unmute with the microphone tapped after the audio
processing, no other side, 5 runs) 2.1 dB at the tap with AGC1 on and 0.2 dB with it off,
the microphone's own noise steady (0.3 dB). Fogata's noise suppression is off unless the
user turns it on, so nothing hides it. With AGC1 off the noise held but the speech went out
7 dB lower; M158 has no field trial that moves the processing to AGC2.

The gate is now measured but not applied: the gain holds through the pauses as it was in
the words. The limiter and the rest of the digital gain are as they were. What it costs:
the room's noise in the pauses goes out about 2 dB louder than before, as loud as it is
under the words.

Not measured with the patch. To measure (study notes a10/NOTES.md): the noise's fall in the
pauses at the tap and at the other side (under 0.5 dB expected), the words' level (about
-16 dBFS, as before), interleaved with the library without it.

